From 1dda9689fb65deb297b8d6b9d00fd23c8e92672b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 19:50:53 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 125/162] fix: the scene-referred flip is a rebuild, not a
 patch

Root cause of the intermittent RenderThread EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION (read at
0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) on the first frame after a level reaches RUNNING with Scene Referred saved on.
The faulting module is nvwgf2umx.dll at two fixed code offsets across ASLR bases, i.e. we were
handing D3D11 a binding whose backing resource had just been released or whose view handle was an
index into a device texture that no longer existed. Diagnosis:
scene-notes/research/s8-renderthread-av-diagnosis.md. Decision:
scene-notes/decisions/s8-flip-safety.md approach A (B, C and D recorded and kept).

The flip used to be executed from inside CGraphicsPipelineResources::Update() by
CTexture::ReformatRenderTarget(), which swapped the device texture underneath each of twelve live
render targets while every CTexture* stayed valid. SetDevTexture()'s eDeviceResourceDirty reaches
every CDeviceResourceSet and CDeviceRenderPass holding the texture - but it does NOT reach a
ResourceViewHandle cached in a stage member, a raw D3DUAV*/D3DSurface* looked up earlier in the
frame, or a stage whose re-bind sits behind an IsDirty() key that does not name the target that
moved. Twelve device textures moving on the first frame of a freshly loaded level, with the sun
shaft stage becoming the first consumer of $DisplayTargetScaled[0] in the same frame and the shader
cache cold, is exactly the window for a stale binding to reach the driver.

- Formats are now decided where the targets are CREATED. CreateHDRMaps() already resolved the main
  and satellite formats from the switch; CreatePostFXMaps() now does the same for the five scaled
  display targets through ResolveDisplayTargetScaledFormat(). Init(), a resolution change and a
  level load's clear-and-recreate therefore all build the targets in the format the switch asks for,
  with nothing left to reformat. That alone removes the crash's own repro: loading a level with the
  switch already on no longer contains a flip at all.

- A runtime flip goes through the engine's own rebuild instead of a bespoke in-place swap.
  CGraphicsPipeline::Update() asks SceneReferredFormatsChanged() first - before
  CGraphicsPipelineResources::Update(), before any stage's Update(), and (in RT_RenderScene) before
  CompileModifiedRenderObjects() and Execute(), so nothing is prepared or bound yet. When it says
  yes, RecreateSceneReferredFormats() pushes the new format in with CTexture::Invalidate(-1, -1,
  wanted) - CE's own primitive for "this render target's format moved", needed because
  CreateRenderTarget() silently discards the format argument of an already-formatted texture - and
  re-runs CreateHDRMaps() and CreatePostFXMaps() at the current resource size, the same two calls
  CGraphicsPipelineResources::Resize() makes. Update() then broadcasts Resize(renderWidth,
  renderHeight) to every stage exactly as CGraphicsPipeline::Resize() does. That broadcast is the
  half the in-place reformat was missing: it is what stops a stage keeping a handle into a device
  texture that has been released. All eight stage Resize() overrides were read first - each either
  reallocates its own resources or early-outs on an unchanged size, so a same-size call is safe.

- CTexture::ReformatRenderTarget() is deleted along with UpdateHDRMainFormats(),
  UpdateHDRSatelliteFormats() and UpdateDisplayTargetScaledFormats(). Nothing else used them.

Two independent defects of the same shape, found by the same audit:

- CAutoExposureStage::ExecuteMeterHistogram() re-bound its output UAV only when
  pass.IsDirty(sourceTexID, histogramTexID) fired. A level load gives the same CTexture a new device
  texture WITHOUT changing its texture ID, so the compute pass kept the previous ResourceViewHandle
  - an index into the old device texture's view container. Whether that bit depended on whether
  GetOrCreateResourceViewHandle happened to return the same index on the new device texture, which
  is what made the fault intermittent. The dirty key now also names both device-texture addresses
  (as uintptr_t; CRenderPassBase::IsDirty static_asserts on non-pointer trivial types).

- CRendererResources' s_szSceneReferredPipelineName stored the caller's
  CGraphicsPipeline::GetUniqueIdentifierName().c_str() - a pointer into a std::string owned by a
  pipeline the static outlives. Read under r_HDRDebug 1, so not this crash, but a real
  use-after-free on any level unload or viewport close. It is a string that owns its characters now.

Byte-identical with the switch off: ResolveDisplayTargetScaledFormat() returns
GetLDRFormat(true) - the stock value - so a stock resize and a stock level load build exactly the
formats they built before, and SceneReferredFormatsChanged() is false on every frame, so
CGraphicsPipeline::Update() reaches m_pipelineResources.Update() having done sixteen
GetDstFormat() reads and nothing else.

